# child_repo_10_LOC (Level 2) - example environment file
#
# A tracked TEMPLATE, not a live configuration. It documents every environment
# variable the Level 2 /health server reads. There are exactly two, both below.
#
# Nothing parses this file at run time: no dotenv package is installed, because
# this composition carries zero third-party dependencies. `health.py` reads
# `os.environ` directly, so a variable takes effect only once something has
# exported it. Set the values explicitly - inline for one run, or exported for a
# shell session:
#
#     HEALTH_PORT=8100 HEALTH_HOST=127.0.0.1 python server.py
#
#     export HEALTH_PORT=8100
#     export HEALTH_HOST=127.0.0.1
#     python server.py
#     curl -i http://127.0.0.1:8100/health
#
# Do NOT source a dotenv file to load these (`. ./.env`, `set -a; . ./.env`).
# Sourcing executes the file as shell code, so a stray backtick or `$(...)` in a
# file you did not write - or a typo in one you did - runs as a command. If you
# keep a local `.env` copy for reference, read the values out of it and set them
# with the explicit forms above; never commit it. If you do want a file applied
# without a shell, `docker run --env-file .env` is the safe form: that parser reads
# NAME=VALUE lines literally, with no substitution, expansion or interpretation of
# `$`, quotes or `;`, so nothing in the file can execute. This tier ships a
# Dockerfile, so the route is already available.
#
# Resolution precedence for HEALTH_PORT and HEALTH_HOST, highest first. This is
# THIS tier's chain - the Level 2, Python chain - and its middle link names this
# repository's own serving document:
#
#     environment variable  ->  config/health.json  ->  compiled-in literal
#
# The three-link SHAPE is uniform across the composition; the middle link's file
# is not, and the difference is worth stating rather than glossing. Level 1 reads
# its own `config/health.json`, as this tier does, because both runtimes parse
# JSON natively. Level 3 has no such document at all: its runtime ships no JSON
# parser, so its serving source is an `application.properties` file read from the
# classpath instead - and, for the same zero-dependency reason, it has no
# `.env.example` either, documenting its two overrides inside that properties file
# and in its own README. That asymmetry is deliberate. Assuming otherwise sends an
# operator looking for a file that tier does not have.
#
# The literal fallback is required, not optional: it guarantees /health still
# serves a valid response when `config/health.json` is missing - from a container
# image, say - which is precisely the failure a health endpoint has to survive.
# The literals in `health.py` are the same 8000 and 0.0.0.0 shown below, so a
# missing configuration file degrades to identical behaviour rather than failure.
#
# The chain degrades one link at a time, so "set but unusable" behaves like
# "unset": `HEALTH_PORT=` and `HEALTH_PORT=not-a-number` both fall through to
# `config/health.json` and then to the literal. The process starts either way - a
# typo in the environment can never stop this endpoint answering. A usable port
# must denote a whole number from 1 to 65535, so `HEALTH_PORT=-1`,
# `HEALTH_PORT=65536` and `HEALTH_PORT=8.000` fall through for the same reason -
# and so, deliberately, does `HEALTH_PORT=0`.
#
# `HEALTH_PORT=0` is one of those unusable values rather than a special one. Only
# 1-65535 is accepted, so a configured zero falls through and the listener binds
# 8000 rather than a port the kernel chose: an endpoint on an unpredictable port
# cannot be reached by anything configured in advance. All three tiers refuse a
# configured zero identically, and the same rule governs the `port` value inside
# `config/health.json`, so editing that file cannot smuggle a zero past it either.
# (Port 0 is still legal as a bind-time *argument*,
# `server.create_server(port=0)`, which is how the test suite binds without
# colliding with a real server - a Python call, deliberately not reachable here.)
#
# That chain governs these two listener values and nothing else:
#
#   * `name` and `version` are read once at import from `pyproject.toml`, this
#     tier's identity manifest, with no environment step. Identity is declared by
#     the repository, not injected by whoever starts the process, so do not add
#     APP_NAME or APP_VERSION: nothing reads them.
#   * The resource path `/health` and the `status` value `UP` are frozen contract
#     constants compiled into `health.py`. `config/health.json` restates both so
#     an operator can see the whole served shape in one place, but a value there
#     that differs from the literal is rejected - the endpoint keeps serving
#     `/health` and `UP`, and `server.py` prints one warning naming the rejected
#     value. There is no HEALTH_PATH and no HEALTH_STATUS variable, and neither
#     may be added: a settable path would move the endpoint away from where a
#     probe looks for it, and a settable status would let a deployment misreport
#     its own health.
#   * This tier prefixes both of its variable names. The apex application uses
#     the bare `HOST` and `PORT` for its own listener, and nothing in this
#     repository reads either - setting them here has no effect. The asymmetry is
#     deliberate and must not be "harmonized".
#
# No further variable belongs in this file: this application has no data store,
# no sign-in, no TLS to terminate and no logging framework. Every value below is
# a plain, non-sensitive default, and because this file is committed it must stay
# that way.
#
# The normative definition of the endpoint and of these values is
# `docs/health-endpoint.md`, in the apex repository of this composition.

# TCP port the /health listener accepts connections on. Default: 8000, this
# tier's reserved port in the composition, so this application can run beside its
# siblings on one host without a collision.
HEALTH_PORT=8000

# Address the /health listener binds to; validated, then handed unchanged to the
# `ThreadingHTTPServer` constructor. Default: 0.0.0.0, meaning every local
# interface, so a published container port stays reachable from outside the host.
# Set it to 127.0.0.1 to confine the listener to loopback during development. An
# IPv6 literal works too - `::1` or `::` - and the address family is selected from
# the literal, so no second setting is needed.
#
# 0.0.0.0 is a BIND address only and never a destination: a probe always
# addresses http://127.0.0.1:<HEALTH_PORT>/health, because a health probe asserts
# the state of *this* process.
HEALTH_HOST=0.0.0.0
